  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThis must have been discussed before, but since I couldn't find anything, here goes…\n\nIt seems there are legit cases where content is semantically meant to just be …big. Not a heading, not `<strong.`, not `<em>`phasized, just large. Very similar to `<small>`.\nMore often than not, these are numbers, prices, stats, etc:\n\n<img width=\"956\" height=\"289\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5c0ee320-be99-4205-b0f9-6682410e8015\" />\n\n<img width=\"1168\" height=\"229\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/142f5b94-937e-4918-a6b5-59e373e3c54b\" />\n\n<img width=\"390\" height=\"450\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8d8718ab-34a2-45b4-9721-2d24b0dbfa62\" />\n\n<img width=\"1257\" height=\"249\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3dc22305-18d9-4acc-9670-2279a2492ec8\" />\n\nEssentially, it's a different type of emphasis, but it seems distinct from `<strong>` and `<em>`.\nUnsure what the AT semantics could be, but once there is a well defined element, these could be added later and \"upgrade\" all instances at once.\n\nOk, I’ll show myself out now… 😅🫣",
